-- Record 2,743 transactions the deployment box was never told had died.
--
-- Run on the ingest machine only. These rows do not exist here — they were
-- deleted long ago — and this is the backlog the new tombstone table cannot
-- have caught, because it did not exist when they went. Writing them here is
-- what puts them in the next delta, which is what finally removes them over
-- there. Running this on the deployment box does nothing useful: the trigger
-- writes tombstones for rows it deletes, and the delta carries them.
--
-- Provenance: the exact set difference in `source_row_hash` between the two
-- databases across all 315,188 expenditure rows of the two feeds that carry
-- expenditures, measured on 2026-09-17. Nothing moved the other way, and none
-- of the 2,743 exists here under any attribution. The ids are the deployment
-- box's, which are this machine's originals, because the rows were created
-- here and shipped before they were deleted.
--
-- What they are: a correction moves a committee's payee off a look-alike
-- person node and onto the candidate, the rebuild's mirror rule then deletes
-- the payer's duplicate of money the candidate already filed, and both steps
-- fall between two syncs. Over there the row still points at the person, where
-- mirror collapse can never pair it, so 2,735 of these put $2,597,970 on 286
-- private individuals who never received it. Robin Bartleman's node reads
-- $62,000 there and $0 here.
--
-- `deleted_at` records when this was written down, not when each row died.
-- That is not on file; the rows were gone before anything recorded it.
--
-- Safe to run twice.
INSERT INTO transaction_tombstones (id, source_row_hash, source_id, reason)
